1. THE CASE BIT -- ASCII 1963
   A 0x41 1000001    a 0x61 1100001   differ in bit 5
   Z 0x5a 1011010    z 0x7a 1111010   differ in bit 5
   chr(ord('A') ^ 0x20) = 'a'
   The committee laid the two alphabets 0x20 apart so a teleprinter
   could shift case by flipping one wire. The scar: every program that
   still does case with XOR breaks on the first non-ASCII letter, and
   'ß'.upper() is two characters, which no bit flip can produce.

2. \r\n -- THE CARRIAGE AND THE PLATEN, 1901
   CR returned the carriage; LF advanced the paper. Two motions, two
   characters, because the machine really did do two things -- and a
   Teletype needed the time both took.
   'a\r\nb'.split(chr(10)) -> ['a\r', 'b']
   The scar: half the world kept both bytes and half kept one, so every
   text file crossing between them has an invisible byte at the end of
   every line, and every tool that shows you TEXT hides it.

3. latin-1 AS A BYTE HACK
   bytes(range(256)).decode('latin-1') -> 256 characters, no error
   Latin-1 maps all 256 byte values to code points 0-255, so it is TOTAL
   -- it can never fail. That makes it the standard trick for smuggling
   arbitrary bytes through a text API, and the standard cause of
   mojibake, and the reason a wrong decode is silent rather than loud.

4. .length COUNTING THE WRONG THING
   😀   Python len 1   utf-16 units 2   utf-8 bytes 4   graphemes 1
   Four numbers, four correct answers, one question. The systems that
   answer 2 adopted Unicode while it was still 16 bits wide.

5. THE EXTRACT THAT IS NOT ASCII AT ALL
   EBCDIC is not a rearrangement of ASCII: its letters come in three
   runs with gaps, so 'is this a letter' cannot be a range check and
   sorting bytes does not sort the alphabet.
   EBCDIC cp037   A=0xc1 I=0xc9 J=0xd1 S=0xe2
   ASCII          A=0x41 I=0x49 J=0x4a S=0x53
   Look at I to J in the EBCDIC row: the run breaks. That is the scar a
   mainframe extract still carries into a modern pipeline.

THE POINT OF THE HISTORY
   Not one of those five was a mistake at the time. Each was the right
   answer to a real constraint -- a wire, a platen, a byte budget, a
   16-bit machine word, a punched card. The mess is what you get when
   six good local decisions have to keep working together for sixty
   years, which is also a fair description of any system you maintain.
